'The United Nations Children’s Fund (UNICEF) has advertised a 12-month, home-based consultancy for a Digital Data Analyst to support digital fundraising across the East Asia and Pacific region, and applications close on 2 November 2026. Nepal is not among the locations listed on the vacancy, so Nepal-based applicants should confirm eligibility before applying.',
'संयुक्त राष्ट्र बाल कोष (युनिसेफ) ले पूर्वी एसिया तथा प्रशान्त क्षेत्रको डिजिटल कोष सङ्कलन कार्यमा सहयोग गर्न डिजिटल डाटा एनालिस्टका लागि १२ महिने, घरबाट काम गर्न सकिने परामर्श सेवा विज्ञापन गरेको छ र आवेदन दिने अन्तिम मिति २ नोभेम्बर २०२६ हो। रिक्तिमा सूचीबद्ध स्थानहरूमा नेपाल परेको छैन, त्यसैले नेपालमा रहेका आवेदकले आवेदन दिनुअघि योग्यता पुष्टि गर्नुपर्छ।',
$$<p>The United Nations Children’s Fund (UNICEF) has advertised a 12-month, home-based consultancy for a Digital Data Analyst to support digital fundraising across the East Asia and Pacific (EAP) region, and applications close on 2 November 2026. The vacancy, job number 596573, was advertised on 26 October 2026, and the work sits with UNICEF’s Global Data and Insights team. One point needs attention at the start: the vacancy lists its locations as the United States, Belgium, Denmark, France, Hungary, Italy, Japan, the Republic of Korea, Switzerland, the United Kingdom, Germany, Spain and Sweden, with Rome as the duty station. Nepal is not on that list, so applicants based in Nepal should confirm eligibility with UNICEF before preparing an application. The full vacancy, including its terms of reference, is on the <a href="https://jobs.unicef.org/en-us/job/596573/digital-data-analyst-eap-region-consultancy-private-fr-partnership-pfp-rome-italy-remote-12-months-international">official UNICEF careers page</a>.</p>
<h2>At a glance</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Organisation</th><td>United Nations Children’s Fund (UNICEF)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Post</th><td>Digital Data Analyst, EAP Region Consultancy, job number 596573</td></tr>
<tr><th>Contract</th><td>Consultancy, 12 months</td></tr>
<tr><th>Working arrangement</th><td>Home-based, with travel</td></tr>
<tr><th>Duty station</th><td>Rome, Italy</td></tr>
<tr><th>Locations listed</th><td>United States, Belgium, Denmark, France, Hungary, Italy, Japan, Republic of Korea, Switzerland, United Kingdom, Germany, Spain, Sweden</td></tr>
<tr><th>Advertised</th><td>26 October 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>Deadline</th><td>2 November 2026, Western Europe Standard Time</td></tr>
<tr><th>Qualification</th><td>Bachelor’s degree in a relevant field, plus 5 years of data and analytics experience</td></tr>
<tr><th>Languages</th><td>Fluency in English and Spanish required</td></tr>
<tr><th>Fee</th><td>Not announced; a separate financial proposal is required</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>What the consultant will do</h2>
<p>The consultancy supports UNICEF’s Individual Giving team, which the vacancy says generates more than USD 1 billion a year and is carrying out a digital transformation of how it acquires donors, keeps them and engages supporters. The consultant will help build advanced data models that can serve as global standards, with particular relevance to the East Asia and Pacific region, and will turn those models into dashboards and machine-learning solutions that managers can use. The work also covers scalable, future-proof data products that are ready for artificial intelligence requirements, so the emphasis is on data that can be reused across campaigns rather than one-off reports. In practice, that means modelling fundraising data, building analytics dashboards, and using evidence from campaigns to improve donor acquisition, retention and engagement. The vacancy says the consultant will work with cloud technologies, and names Google Analytics 4 and BigQuery alongside strong SQL skills and experience with relational and non-relational databases.</p>
<h2>Who can apply</h2>
<p>The minimum education requirement is a bachelor’s degree in Computer Science, Statistics, Mathematics, Information Technology or another related field, the vacancy states. Applicants must also have at least five years of progressively responsible professional experience in data and analytics. Relevant experience named in the vacancy includes data modelling, database management, cloud analytics, dashboard development and digital marketing analytics, and the use of data to improve organisational performance. On skills, the vacancy requires experience with cloud technologies, in particular Google Analytics 4 and BigQuery, strong SQL knowledge, experience with relational and non-relational databases, and strong communication skills, including the ability to explain complex information clearly. Experience building analytics dashboards and using data to drive insights for campaign optimisation is highly desirable. On languages, fluency in both English and Spanish is required, and knowledge of another official United Nations language — Arabic, Chinese, French or Russian — is an asset. The vacancy also lists desirables that go beyond technical skill: a willingness to learn new technologies, an interest in fundraising developments, and the ability to explain marketing solutions and data to non-technical colleagues.</p>
<h2>What to submit</h2>
<p>Applications go through UNICEF’s Talent Management System, and three items are required. First, an up-to-date profile and curriculum vitae that set out education, employment history, technical skills and relevant data analytics work. Second, a cover letter that explains suitability for the consultancy and how the applicant’s experience matches its requirements. Third, a separate financial proposal, prepared using UNICEF’s official financial proposal template, which is linked from the vacancy. The vacancy does not state a consultancy fee or salary, so applicants should not quote any figure from secondary summaries: the fee is set through the financial proposal, and the article does not guess it. Applicants should also read the complete terms of reference, linked from the vacancy, before writing, since it carries the full list of deliverables, milestones and expected outcomes.</p>
<h2>How to apply</h2>
<ol>
<li>Read the full vacancy and its terms of reference on the official UNICEF careers page, and check the location list against your own base.</li>
<li>Confirm that you meet the degree, five-year experience, technical and language requirements, and that you can work home-based with travel.</li>
<li>Update your UNICEF Talent Management System profile and curriculum vitae, with concrete data analytics work and measurable outcomes where you have them.</li>
<li>Write a cover letter tied to this consultancy: SQL, BigQuery, Google Analytics 4, dashboard work and campaign optimisation, rather than general IT experience.</li>
<li>Complete the separate financial proposal using UNICEF’s official template, and check it against the template’s own instructions.</li>
<li>Submit through the <a href="https://secure.dc7.pageuppeople.com/apply/671/gateway/default.aspx?c=apply&lJobID=596573&lJobSourceTypeID=796&sLanguage=en-us">official application portal</a> before the deadline of 2 November 2026, Western Europe Standard Time, and keep a copy of the submitted application.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Apply safely</h2>
<p>UNICEF states that it does not charge a processing fee at any stage of recruitment, selection, hiring, appointment or training, and that it will not ask applicants for bank account information. Only shortlisted candidates will be contacted and advance to the next stage, the vacancy states, and selected candidates will undergo reference and background checks, including verification of academic credentials and employment history. Apply only through the official UNICEF recruitment portal linked above. Anyone asking for payment to secure this consultancy, promising selection, or asking for bank details is not acting for UNICEF. For applicants in Nepal, the practical caution is the location list: the vacancy does not list Nepal among its locations, so confirm eligibility on the official page before paying anything to any intermediary or sharing documents outside the official portal.</p>
